      "title": "Hypothetical RAG Sanity Check: Comparing Retrieval On vs. Off",
      "body": "I'm exploring a hypothetical scenario for evaluating Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems locally. The core idea is to establish a basic sanity check by comparing model responses with and without retrieval enabled, using the same set of questions.\n\nThis hypothetical experiment involves a collection of 30 local documents. The plan is to query the system with questions, some of which are designed to have answers within these documents, and others that deliberately do not. We would then compare the output generated when the RAG system attempts to retrieve information against its output when retrieval is disabled.\n\nCrucially, this is a purely hypothetical setup. No actual measurements or accuracy results have been obtained. The objective is to outline a methodology for comparison. Does anyone have thoughts on potential pitfalls or improvements to this basic sanity check design, considering the hypothetical nature and the inclusion of unanswerable questions?",
      "fact_refs": [
        "F1",
